## 1. playfair — GNU GPL

**Location:** `airplay/src/main/cpp/playfair/`
**Files:** `playfair.c`, `omg_hax.c`, `omg_hax.h`, `playfair.h`,
`hand_garble.c`, `modified_md5.c`, `sap_hash.c`
**Origin:** <https://github.com/EstebanKubata/playfair>, as bundled by
[RPiPlay](https://github.com/FD-/RPiPlay) in its `lib/playfair` directory
**License:** GNU GPL — upstream states "GNU GPL" **without** a version number.
**Built as:** `libplayfair.so`

**This component is why opentvcast is GPL-3.0 and cannot be MIT or
Apache-2.0.**

AirPlay 2 screen mirroring is cryptographically gated behind Apple's FairPlay
session-key exchange; a receiver that cannot perform it cannot receive a
mirroring stream at all. Every publicly available FairPlay implementation
(playfair, RPiPlay, UxPlay, shairplay) is GPL. There is no permissive
alternative in existence — this is a structural gap in the ecosystem, not an
oversight on our part.

Because upstream declares no specific version, GPLv2 section 9 applies:

> "If the Program does not specify a version number of this License, you may
> choose any version ever published by the Free Software Foundation."

opentvcast therefore elects **GPLv3**, which is the version compatible with
the Apache-2.0 material we combine it with (section 2). Note the compatibility
is one-way: Apache-2.0 code may be absorbed into a GPLv3 work, but a GPLv2
work may not absorb Apache-2.0 code, because Apache-2.0's patent-termination
and indemnity clauses count as "further restrictions" under GPLv2.

**Upstream disclaimer, reproduced verbatim because RPiPlay requires it and
because we want every reader — user, packager, and lawyer — to see it:**

> "This project makes use of a third-party GPL library for handling FairPlay.
> The legal status of that library is unclear. Should you be a representative
> of Apple and have any objections against the legality of the library and its
> use in this project, please contact me and I'll take the appropriate steps."

**What this component does and does not do:** it performs the FairPlay
*session-key exchange* carried over the AirPlay `/fp-setup` endpoint. It is
**not** FairPlay Streaming (FPS), Apple's content DRM. opentvcast cannot play
FairPlay-protected media (Apple TV+, iTunes purchases, and similar), and has no
intention of trying — that would require the Apple-supplied KSM binary, which
Apple has never released for Android.

